#: tips.cpp:3
msgid ""
"<p>...that you can search for objects in the sky by their name?<br>\n"
"Use Ctrl+F, the \"Focus->Find Object\" menu item, or the \"Find\"\n"
"Toolbar button.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:9
msgid ""
"<p>...that you can change your Geographic Location?<br>\n"
"Use Ctrl+G, the \"Settings->Set Geographic Location...\" menu item,\n"
"or the \"globe\" Toolbar button.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:15
msgid ""
"<p>...that you can Track an object, so it will always be centered\n"
"in the display?<br>\n"
"Use Ctrl+T, the \"Focus->Track Object\" menu item, or the \"lock\"\n"
"Toolbar button. You can also simply center the object by\n"
"double-clicking on it or selecting \"Center and Track\" from the\n"
"popup menu.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:24
msgid ""
"<p>...that the KStars Handbook includes the AstroInfo Project, a series \n"
"of informative articles about Astronomy?\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:29
msgid ""
"<p>...that you can directly access many of the calculations that\n"
"KStars does behind-the-scenes?<br>\n"
"Open the KStars Astrocalculator by pressing Ctrl+C or by selecting\n"
"the \"Tools->Calculator\" menu item.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:36
msgid ""
"<p>There are three on-screen \"Info Boxes\" which show data related to the\n"
"time/date, your geographic location, and the current central position on "
"the\n"
"sky (the focus).  You can drag these boxes with the mouse, and \"shade\" "
"them\n"
"by double-clicking them to show more (or less) information.  You can hide\n"
"them altogether in the Settings->Info Boxes menu.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:44
msgid ""
"<p>...that the Z key will point the display at the Zenith?\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:48
msgid ""
"<p>...that the N,S,E,W keys will point the display at the North,\n"
"South, East and West points on the Horizon?\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:53
msgid ""
"<p>Click and Drag with the mouse to slew the skymap to a new location.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:57
msgid ""
"<p>Double-click with the mouse to center the display on the location\n"
"of the mouse cursor.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:62
msgid ""
"<p>When you click your mouse in the map, the object in the sky nearest\n"
"the mouse cursor is identified in the status bar.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:67
msgid ""
"<p>Right-click with the mouse to open a popup menu of detailed options\n"
"for a particular object, including links to images and information on\n"
"the Internet.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:73
msgid ""
"<p>The status bar always displays the current Right Ascension, Declination\n"
"coordinates of the mouse cursor.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:78
msgid ""
"<p>...that there are 5 different ways to zoom the display in and out?\n"
"You can use the mouse's scroll wheel, drag the mouse vertically while "
"pressing\n"
"the middle button, use the +/- keys, use the zoom buttons on the toolbar,\n"
"and use the \"Zoom In\" and \"Zoom Out\" items in the \"View\" menu.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:85
msgid ""
"<p>...that you can set the Focus point manually? Press Ctrl+M, or\n"
"use the \"Focus->Set Focus Manually...\" menu item, and enter the desired\n"
"coordinates in the popup window.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:91
msgid ""
"<p>...that you can switch between Equatorial and Horizontal coordinate\n"
"systems? Use the \"View->Coordinates\" menu item, or the buttons\n"
"in the KStars Configuration window.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:97
msgid ""
"<p>...that you can set the Time and Date? <br>\n"
"Use Ctrl+S, the \"Time->Set Time\" menu item, or the \"hourglass\" toolbar\n"
"button.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:103
msgid ""
"<p>...that you can use Ctrl+E or the \"Time->Set Time to Now\" to "
"synchronize\n"
"the simulation clock with your CPU clock?\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:108
msgid ""
"<p>The Spin Box in the Toolbar allows you to adjust the time step\n"
"used by the KStars clock. Setting it to \"1.0 sec\" provides \"real time\".\n"
"Negative values make time run backwards!\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:114
msgid ""
"<p>You can stop and start the clock with the \"Play/Pause\" button\n"
"in the Toolbar, or with the \"Time->Stop/Start Clock\" menu item.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:119
msgid ""
"<p>The deep-sky objects with a special color (the default is Red)\n"
"have extra URL links available in their popup menu.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:124
msgid ""
"<p>By default, stars in KStars are displayed with realistic colors.\n"
"A star's color depends on its temperature; cooler stars are red,\n"
"while hotter stars are blue.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:130
msgid ""
"<p>...that you can add your own custom Object catalogs? Select\n"
"\"Add Catalog\" from the Catalogs tab in the KStars Configuration window.\n"
"See the Handbook for instructions on formatting your catalog file.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:136
msgid ""
"<p>...that you can add your own custom image/information URLs to\n"
"any object? Select \"Add Link...\" from the popup menu.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:141
msgid ""
"<p>...that you can adjust dozens of display options by clicking the\n"
"\"wrench\" Toolbar button, or selecting the \"Settings->Configure "
"KStars...\"\n"
"menu item?\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:147
msgid ""
"<p>...that you can hide the on-screen Info Boxes?\n"
"Select your preferences in the \"Settings->Info Boxes\" menu.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:152
msgid ""
"<p>...that you can hide the Toolbars? Select your preferences\n"
"in the \"Settings->Toolbars\" menu.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:157
msgid ""
"<p>You can easily switch between predefined color schemes by selecting\n"
"the scheme from the \"Settings->Color Schemes\" menu.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:162
msgid ""
"<p>...that you can define your own Geographic Locations? Fill in the\n"
"required fields in the \"Change Location\" Dialog and then press the\n"
"\"Add to List\" button. Your Locations will be available in all\n"
"future sessions.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:169
msgid ""
"<p>...that you can define your own Color Schemes? Adjust the colors\n"
"in the Configuration Window's \"Colors\" Tab, and then press the \"Save\n"
"Current Colors\" button. Your Color Scheme will appear in the list in\n"
"all future sessions.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:176
msgid ""
"<p>The Advanced tab of the KStars Configuration window allows you to "
"fine-tune\n"
"the behavior of KStars. You can specify whether to correct for atmospheric\n"
"refraction, and whether to use animated slewing. You can also specify which\n"
"objects are hidden while the display is in motion.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:183
msgid ""
"<p>KStars displays 40,000 stars, 13,000 deep-sky objects, 88 "
"constellations,\n"
"8 planets, the Sun, the Moon, and the Milky Way.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:188
msgid ""
"<p>The line in the sky that the Sun and all the Planets seem to follow\n"
"is called the Ecliptic.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:193
msgid ""
"<p>Object positions in KStars include the effects of precession, nutation,\n"
"aberration, atmospheric refraction, and light travel time (for planets).\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:198
msgid ""
"<p>The nearest star to the Sun is Rigel Kentaurus (alpha Centauri).\n"
"The brightest star in the sky is Sirius (alpha Canis Majoris).\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:203
msgid ""
"<p>The large group of galaxies between Leo, Virgo and Coma Berenices\n"
"is called the Virgo Cluster of Galaxies.\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:208
msgid ""
"<p>The large group of clusters and nebulae near the south celestial pole\n"
"are objects in the Large Magellanic Cloud, which is a dwarf galaxy in\n"
"orbit around the Milky Way!\n"
msgstr ""

#: tips.cpp:214
msgid ""
"<p>"
"The Messier Catalog is a list of 110 of the brightest non-stellar objects \n"
"in the sky.  It includes such famous objects as the Orion Nebula (M 42), the "
"\n"
"Andromeda Galaxy (M 31), and the Pleiades (M 45).\n"
msgstr ""

